;;; A written fact of `eq` at one argument is stored — **W61**, regression
;;; `fact-eq-arity` (`corpus/regressions.toml`), found beside W60.
;;;
;;;
;;; ── What this program is ───────────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; One fact `(p a)`, and a fact of `eq` written with one argument, `(eq a)`.
;;;
;;; ── The answer, by hand ────────────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; `eq` is the relation of equality, between exactly two values, whose facts
;;; are computed and never stored (Q-M1e2.26), and a written fact of it is
;;; checked as a conclusion with no premise (Q-M1f.33). One argument is no fact
;;; of equality, true or false, and the grammar refuses `(eq ?x)` as a premise
;;; and as a conclusion: the program is refused at load, one line naming the
;;; fact, and every command that loads it exits 1 (`ein test`: 2).
;;;
;;;
;;; ── What the engine answers ────────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; The program loads and stores `(eq a)` as an ordinary fact (`ein saturate
;;; FILE --dump`), the check of a written `eq` fact reading two arguments
;;; only; `eq` holds no candidate, and R is two states, ∅ and `{(p z)}`, exit
;;; 0 (`ein solve FILE` and `ein oracle FILE` at `ee3da657`, release and
;;; debug alike).
;;;
;;; ── What the engine answers now ────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; The loader refuses the program now: every command that loads it exits 1 with
;;; one line, naming the fact (`ein test`: 2), in either build, where it stored
;;; `(eq a)`. The regression's `now` is that refusal; the section above is the
;;; record of the answer it replaced.

(relation p Node :why "{?1} is a p")
(p a)
(hit z)
(eq a)
(query :goal (p ?x))
